  "justification": "The redesign addresses every Well-Architected pillar. Operational Excellence: the ASG remains the scaling unit (N6) but is retuned to m6g.large instances sized against the stated 70%-CPU capacity table, so headroom and scale-in/out thresholds are explicit and predictable rather than guessed; ElastiCache and a second NAT gateway are added as discrete, independently manageable components rather than ad-hoc fixes. Security is preserved and strengthened: compute stays in private subnets behind the ALB, outbound traffic still egresses only through NAT, and database credentials/network paths are unchanged, while doubling NAT gateways removes a shared failure/bottleneck point without widening the attack surface. Reliability is the central improvement: the original design had only one NAT gateway (a single point of failure for all outbound traffic from both AZs) and a minimum of two t3.micro web instances with no demonstrated capacity to absorb a traffic spike. The proposal keeps at least two instances across two AZs at all times (N2), adds a second NAT gateway per AZ, and retains Multi-AZ RDS with automated backups and point-in-time recovery, giving an RPO well under 5 minutes (continuous transaction-log shipping) and an RTO far under 1 hour (automatic failover typically completes in 1-2 minutes) — satisfying N3 with margin. Performance Efficiency: capacity planning is now driven by the stated per-instance throughput table instead of assumption. Origin tier must sustain 750 req/s at peak (N1); three m6g.large instances provide 960 req/s of headroom (≈22% margin over absolute need), while baseline traffic (75 req/s after cache offload) is comfortably covered by the two-instance HA floor. Graviton (m6g.large) was chosen over equivalently-priced-per-capacity t3 instances because it has the best price-per-unit-capacity in the given price sheet (0.077/320 ≈ 0.000241 vs ≈0.00026 for the t3 family), and because fewer, larger instances reduce orchestration/monitoring overhead versus an alternative using many t3.micro/t3.small instances, even though that extreme would be marginally cheaper — a clear Operational Excellence/Cost trade-off made deliberately. ElastiCache is added to absorb repeated read queries (e.g., photo metadata, user profiles) during the 60 peak hours/month, protecting the database tier and smoothing latency without resizing RDS further. Cost Optimization: every component was right-sized to the actual workload rather than left at original Stage-3 levels. The ALB LCU estimate is tied to measured origin traffic (not flat guesswork), CloudFront costs are the dominant line item because N4 fixes CDN demand at 2,000 GB and 40M requests — this is unavoidable and correctly costed rather than underestimated as in the original BOM (which only covered 200GB/5M requests). The total proposed bill is $585.03/month, comfortably inside the $750 budget (N5), leaving margin for traffic variance. Sustainability: Graviton processors are measurably more power-efficient per vCPU than x86 t3 instances, and right-sizing (avoiding permanently over-provisioned capacity, using autoscaling to shed capacity in the 670 non-peak hours) reduces idle compute and therefore energy draw relative to a static fleet sized for peak at all times.",
  "comparison": "The original architecture was undersized and had single points of failure: one NAT gateway served both AZs, t3.micro instances (40 req/s capacity each) could only provide 160 req/s even at the stated max of 4 instances — far short of the 750 req/s peak origin demand now required, meaning the site would have failed to scale under N1's load. Its RDS tier was Multi-AZ (good) but undersized (db.t3.micro) for the new query volume, and its CloudFront/S3 costing reflected a much smaller 200GB/5M-request workload, understating true demand versus N4's 2,000GB/40M-request requirement. The proposed architecture fixes capacity (m6g.large instances sized against the official capacity table, comfortably covering 750 req/s peak with headroom), adds a second NAT gateway to remove a reliability bottleneck, upsizes the database to db.t3.medium while keeping Multi-AZ failover and point-in-time recovery (meeting the 5-minute RPO / 1-hour RTO explicitly), and adds ElastiCache to protect the database from read pressure at peak. Monthly cost rises from $133.21 to $585.03 — a large absolute increase, but this mostly reflects the genuinely larger CloudFront workload mandated by N4 (210 of the increase) and properly-sized compute/database/NAT redundancy, not waste; the result still lands well under the $750 budget ceiling (N5), unlike the original design, which was cheap only because it could not actually serve the stated workload or meet the stated availability and recovery objectives."
