  "justification": "The redesign is evaluated against all six Well-Architected pillars.\n\nOperational Excellence: capacity is now managed by Auto Scaling policies tied to request/CPU metrics instead of a static 2-4 t3.micro fleet, so the fleet reacts to the defined peak/off-peak pattern automatically. Multi-AZ RDS failover is handled by AWS, removing manual recovery runbooks that the original architecture implicitly required.\n\nSecurity: the private-subnet placement of compute and database tier is unchanged, and traffic still terminates TLS at CloudFront/ALB. Routing S3 traffic through a VPC Gateway Endpoint instead of the NAT Gateway keeps photo data off the public NAT path, narrowing the exposure surface versus the original design.\n\nReliability: this is the pillar most clearly failed by the original architecture. Its ASG tops out at 4 t3.micro instances (4x40=160 req/s), far short of the 750 req/s origin demand created by N1 peak load - under real traffic it would have returned 5xx errors for most of each peak window. The proposed fleet provides 960 req/s of capacity at peak (3 of 4 m6g.large instances active, 78% headroom utilization) and always keeps >=2 instances across 2 AZs, satisfying N2. The original's single NAT Gateway was also a latent single point of failure for all outbound/private connectivity; the proposal adds a second NAT Gateway in the second AZ to eliminate that risk. RDS keeps Multi-AZ synchronous replication (already present in the original) which delivers near-zero data loss (RPO well under N3's 5-minute bar) and automatic failover typically inside 60-120 seconds (comfortably inside the 1-hour RTO in N3); point-in-time recovery with 7-day retention adds a second, independent recovery path.\n\nPerformance Efficiency: m6g.large (Graviton2) was selected because it has the lowest cost-per-request-second of any tier on the price sheet (about $0.00024 per req/s-hour versus $0.00026 for the t3 family) while also avoiding the CPU-credit exhaustion